g formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(1)=⟨0,4⟩,$  since the point $P(0,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2}),-\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =⟨-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-\\pi )^{2}}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{3\\pi }{\\pi }=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=1$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }⟨0,-\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)+R(1)N(1) & =⟨0,4⟩+3⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369963 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369964 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369965 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369966 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369967 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1041 "selection" 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369966 13194139535847 true] [17592186369962 1110 #uuid "363911d0-eb2a-5ef2-9599-893a67972d43" 13194139535847 true] [17592186369963 1034 "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $3$" 13194139535847 true] [17592186369963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535847 true] [